\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $M_1,M_2$ be smooth closed oriented $4$-manifolds that are orientation-preservingly homeomorphic. For $k\ge0$, let $M_i\#_k(S^2\times S^2)$ mean stabilization by the connected sum with $k$ standard copies of the product; for $k=0$ it is $M_i$. Equivalence of stabilized manifolds means oriented diffeomorphism. The manifolds need not be simply connected. The related twisted stabilization uses the nontrivial oriented $S^2$ bundle over $S^2$, diffeomorphic to $\mathbb{CP}^2\#\overline{\mathbb{CP}}^{\,2}$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
(a) Is $M_1\#(S^2\times S^2)$ always diffeomorphic to $M_2\#(S^2\times S^2)$? (b) Is there a single integer $n>0$, independent of the pair, for which
\[
M_1\#_n(S^2\times S^2)\cong M_2\#_n(S^2\times S^2)
\]
for every homeomorphic closed oriented pair $M_1,M_2$? The source also asks the same one-stabilization and universal-bound questions with the twisted $S^2$ bundle in place of $S^2\times S^2$. The bound in (b) must be uniform across all pairs; the known existence of a pair-dependent stabilization number is weaker. \href{https://math.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/surv-295-ruberman-watermarked-author-pdf.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does one product stabilization make every homeomorphic closed oriented four-manifold pair diffeomorphic, or is there at least a universal finite number of stabilizations? Ask the corresponding questions for twisted-sphere-bundle stabilization as well.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2883 (KP-4.7). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] R. İnanç Baykur, Robion C. Kirby and Daniel Ruberman (editors), \emph{K3: A New Problem List in Low-Dimensional Topology}, Mathematical Surveys and Monographs 295 (2026), Problem 4.7, p. 196 and following remarks; original author version. \url{https://math.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/surv-295-ruberman-watermarked-author-pdf.pdf}.
\end{description}
